Caleb McDaniel, Open Notebook History, May 2013
-	Opens with the question of “What would happen if historians made their research notes public? What would it look like to make our notebooks “open source”?
-	Poses the question of: what would it look like to make our notebooks digital and “open source” from the very beginning of a project?
-	Open Notebook Science (ONS) is the practice of putting one’s entire lab notebook online, so that other researchers have access not just to a scientist’s publications, but to the underlying data, methods, and experimental results that drive research projects forward
-	If all Open Notebook scholarship promises is more scrutiny from more potential fraud-finders, it is hard to see its attraction. However, a better way to frame the appeal of open notebooks is less in terms of fear and more in terms of what Black called “the better parts of academics’ nature” – our scholarly values of open intellectual exchange, integrity, and honesty
-	The truth is that we often don’t realize the value of what we have until someone else sees it
-	By inviting others to see our work in progress, we also open new avenues of interpretation, uncover new linkages between things we would otherwise have persisted in seeing as unconnected, and create new opportunities for collaboration with fellow travelers.
-	The link is a powerful way to “symbolize ideas about relationship” and thus to symbolize the act of higher-order cognition itself

Open Science in Archaeology, Sep 2017
-	We believe that there is much to be gained, both for individual researchers and for the discipline, from broader application of open science practices
-	Identified three element of open science: open access, open data, and open methods
-	Open Access
o	Open access typically refers to permanent online access to the full text of scholarly work, especially publication, without charge to readers or libraries
o	Open access publications typically achieve increased impact by being cited more frequently and receiving more media coverage
-	Open Data
o	Open data means open access to datasets, which can take on many forms including spreadsheets of artifact measurements or a GUS layer of site locations and attributes – typically the information used to make the summary tables and plots that appear in reports and publications
o	Opening data requires consideration of intellectual property ownership, and potential for harm to people and cultural heritage form the misuse of the data or release of sensitive information
-	Open Methods
o	Open methods are methods of data collection, analysis, and visualization that are available for inspection and reuse by the public
o	It can include empirical methods and computational and statistical methods
o	Open methods are important for improving the reproducibility of research; that is, the ability to redo a study, with the same materials and methods, and get the same result
-	Open science practices support this goal by encouraging archaeologists to conduct research that is transparent, reusable, and easily accessible
-	Some archaeologists may fear the limitations to publication potential that could result from others using their open data and code, the possibility that their materials may be used without citation, and the risk that competitors may gain an advantage. Our view is that these risks have always been present in the traditional research practices of scholarly communication and peer review, and that open science licensing and citation practices effectively mitigate them. Moreover, because sharing of data and code enables and encourages collaborative research, more open science practices can even increase the potential for new research (and publications) with extant data—an important benefit to junior researchers in particular. Overall, we believe any costs for the practice of open science are well worth the many substantial benefits it brings to archaeologists and the archaeological community.

